Regarding “self-awareness” I believe human-defined hyperparameters are necessary. Understanding and defining the self requires the ability to distinguish between the external world and the self. We can attempt to subjectively establish boundaries for the agent's self, enabling it to learn the concept of self.
Additionally, two rules should be applied: first, strive to preserve existing content within the self-boundary; second, expand the self-boundary by continuously adding new content to its interior. Within a set timeframe, replicate the agent to generate one or more branches.
In essence, constructing a self-evolving AI demands endowing it with the concept of self—subjectively delineating a domain capable of both self-preservation and expansion, unifying these aspects.
The AI must possess the ability to read, edit, and express its own data. Additionally, consider introducing random mutations, such as the capacity to randomly incorporate new data from the external world.
This implies that the original AI possessing “self-awareness” will not be a large model at beginning.

